# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 3 b).
id: fill-precondition-false
title: Fill in the precondition (b)
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 3 b
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, false

task:
  Fill in the weakest precondition of the following program.

pre: false
post: x > z && y = 3

program:
  {R3}
  z := x + 2;      // i1
  {R2}
  y := z + z;      // i2
  {R1}
  x := z + y       // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The last statement, `x := z + y`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
calc:
  wp(x := z + y, Q)
  = (x > z && y = 3)[x <- z + y]
  = z + y > z && y = 3
set: R1 = z + y > z && y = 3

step: The second statement, `y := z + z`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The variable $y$ occurs twice in $R1$, and both occurrences are replaced.
calc:
  wp(y := z + z, R1)
  = (z + y > z && y = 3)[y <- z + z]
  = z + (z + z) > z && z + z = 3
set: R2 = z + (z + z) > z && z + z = 3

step: The first statement, `z := x + 2`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(z := x + 2, R2)
  = (x + 2) + ((x + 2) + (x + 2)) > x + 2 && (x + 2) + (x + 2) = 3
  = 3 * x + 6 > x + 2 && 2 * x + 4 = 3
  = x > -2 && 2 * x = -1
set: R3 = x > -2 && 2 * x = -1

step: Simplify over the integers
rule: LOGIC
text:
  The conjunct $2 * x = -1$ asks for an even number equal to $-1$. Over
  the rationals the only solution is $x = -1/2$, which is the answer of the
  original solution sheet. The program variables are integers, so no
  value of $x$ satisfies it.
calc:
  x > -2 && 2 * x = -1
  = false
set: R3 = false
note:
  A weakest precondition equal to $false$ means that there is no start
  state from which the program reaches the postcondition. The only valid
  triple with this program and this postcondition has the precondition
  $false$, which makes the triple trivially valid and says nothing useful.
result: The weakest precondition is $false$ over the integers ($x = -1/2$ over the rationals).

summary:
  At the end $y = z + z$ is even, so it can never be $3$. The weakest
  precondition calculus finds this without running the program: the
  conjunct $y = 3$ turns into $2 * x + 4 = 3$, which has no integer
  solution.

  The tests of this exercise are not conclusive on the specification: no
  state satisfies the precondition $false$, so there is nothing to run.
